findings.body:
This regression test can pass even when the bug is present. FluentAssertions' `NotThrow<OptionsValidationException>()` only fails on that one exception type and passes if any other exception is thrown.

The options are resolved from a bare `builder.Services.BuildServiceProvider()`, not the provider the AppHost builds. If Aspire's check for unregistered health check keys needs a service that only exists after `Build()`, or reports the problem with a different exception, or only runs at startup, this test passes with `.WithHealthCheck(name)` still in place. A future change that re-adds an unregistered key would then go uncaught. Nothing shows this test failing against the old code, and CLAUDE.md asks for the failing test first.

Fix: assert directly on what the test name promises, which doesn't depend on how Aspire validates. Keep the resource builder, then:
```csharp
HealthCheckServiceOptions options = provider.GetRequiredService<IOptions<HealthCheckServiceOptions>>().Value;
IEnumerable<string> registered = options.Registrations.Select(r => r.Name);
mongodb.Resource.Annotations.OfType<HealthCheckAnnotation>().Select(a => a.Key).Should().BeSubsetOf(registered);
```
At minimum, use `act.Should().NotThrow()`. Then confirm the test goes red with `.WithHealthCheck(name)` restored.
